Rooms has no HTTP API and no message format of its own. Everything a client
does is a call on an object it holds, carried by glowcap over
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn)'s CapTP. The server publishes
exactly one object by name, `directory`. Every other reference a client ever
uses was handed back by that object, or by an object it led to. This page
lays the whole protocol out as it is: objects, the calls they answer, what
comes back, and how long each thing lives.

If OCapN is new to you, [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) explains references,
promises, and introductions from the ground up, and
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers locators and durable links.

# The objects

Each card is one object: how you come to hold it, the calls it answers, and
what it hands back. Everything it hands back is a card of its own, so the
tiers read as a chain from the one published name down to a single line of
text. Cool cards live on the server. Warm cards are handed to you and are
yours to use or pass on. The inbox is the one you make yourself.

```=html
<div class="objects">
<p class="objects-tier">Published by the server</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">directory</p>
<p class="object-from">Fetch it by name. The one published object.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>open</code> <code>enter</code> <code>list</code> <code>watch</code> <code>register</code> <code>introduce</code> <code>offerInbox</code> <code>link</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>room</b>, a <b>name</b>, another member's <b>inbox</b>, or a room link.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">inspect and peering</p>
<p class="object-from">Each is a secret the server prints when started with <code>--diagnostics</code> or <code>--peering</code>. Fetch it like the directory.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>snapshot</code> <code>publishDurableRoom</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back what the server holds right now, or a lease that answers <code>ready</code> and <code>revoke</code>.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by the directory</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="cool">
<p class="object-name">room</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>open</code> or <code>enter</code>, or a room link.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>subscribe</code> <code>called</code> <code>private</code> <code>roomPolicy</code> <code>managedRoomIdentity</code> <code>link</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>seat</b>, a <b>topic</b>, or a room link.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">name</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>register</code>, with the name you want.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>voucher</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back proof that you hold this name, for a second connection.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">manager</p>
<p class="object-from">The management link <code>openManaged</code> hands you.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>link</code> <code>giveSteward</code> <code>revokeSteward</code> <code>revokeRoomLink</code> <code>setRoomPolicy</code> <code>unregisterRoom</code> <code>managedRoomIdentity</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a reissued room link or the room's <b>steward</b>.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">steward</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>giveSteward</code> on the manager, or the operator's admin page. Minted once per room; asking again hands back the same one.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>kick</code> <code>governs</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. A member is asked to leave.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by a room</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">seat</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>subscribe</code>, with your <b>inbox</b>.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>post</code> <code>postFiles</code> <code>attach</code> <code>history</code> <code>typing</code> <code>away</code> <code>leave</code> <code>membershipKind</code> <code>mintInvitation</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back a <b>line</b>, an <b>upload</b>, an <b>invitation</b>, or the lines the room kept.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">topic</p>
<p class="object-from">The third slot of <code>subscribe</code>'s answer, for participants.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>setTopic</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. The room tells everyone.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Handed back by your seat</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">line</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>post</code>. The line you just said.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>edit</code> <code>withdraw</code> <code>refile</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back nothing. The room tells everyone through their inbox.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">upload and blob</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>attach</code> hands you one upload per file. The room hands everyone a blob for it.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>write</code> <code>discard</code> <code>read</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Bytes go in through the upload and come out of the blob, from an offset, up to a length.</p>
</article>
<article class="object" data-tone="warm">
<p class="object-name">invitation</p>
<p class="object-from"><code>mintInvitation</code> on a seat.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>claimInvitation</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Hands back the <b>room</b>, exactly once. Then <code>subscribe</code> for a seat.</p>
</article>
</div>
<p class="objects-tier">Yours</p>
<div class="objects-row">
<article class="object" data-tone="own">
<p class="object-name">inbox</p>
<p class="object-from">You make it, and hand it to rooms and to the directory. Rooms call <code>notify</code> on it; other members call the rest.</p>
<p class="object-calls"><code>notify</code> <code>dm</code> <code>directReady</code> <code>handRoom</code> <code>offerRoom</code> <code>handArchive</code> <code>offerArchive</code></p>
<p class="object-back">Receives every line a room sends you, and whatever another member hands you directly.</p>
</article>
</div>
</div>
```

Two things about these cards are the whole design. Nothing checks who is
calling: a member without the line reference cannot form the `edit` at all,
so there is nothing to check. And a reference can be handed on. Give your
line's reference to someone and they can take the line down; give the topic
reference to a bot and it can keep the topic, without the room learning what
a bot is.

# What the room tells you

The room calls `notify` on your inbox. After the text, a line carries a tag
that says what kind of thing it is. A client that does not know a tag skips
it and draws the line anyway, so the room can say more over time without
every client being rebuilt first.

| Tag | Then | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `line` | id, then any file records | An ordinary line, under the id this room knows it by |
| `edited` | id | That line now reads as this text |
| `refiled` | id, then the new file records | That line's files are now exactly these |
| `withdrawn` | id | That line is taken back |
| `roster` | names | Who is here, sent when you arrive |
| `join`, `leave` | name | Someone came or went |
| `typing` | name, on or off | Someone is typing, never stored |
| `away` | name, reason | Someone stepped away; an empty reason means they are back |
| `topic` | text | What the room is about; empty means taken down |
| `line-bytes` | a number | The longest line this room will carry |
| `listed`, `unlisted` | | Whether this room is on the public list, sent when you arrive |
| `membershipEnded` | | This room no longer holds the seat behind your inbox |

# How long each thing lives

| What | Lives as long as | To get it again |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A room entrance reference | Your connection to the server | `enter` by name, or redeem the room's link |
| A seat | Until you `leave`, are kicked, or disconnect | `subscribe` again |
| A line reference | Your connection | It is not reissued; hand it on if someone else should hold it |
| A room link | Until the manager releases it | It is a string; keep it |
| An invitation | One use | `mintInvitation` again |
| A direct connection | Until either side closes it | Ask the directory to introduce you again |
| A steward | Until the room's authority is rotated | Ask the manager again; it hands back the same one |
| An admin secret | Until the server restarts without a data directory | It is written into the data directory |

A live reference dies with its connection. When room entry has to outlive that,
Rooms asks the server for a durable link, an OCapN sturdyref. The link preserves
the authority to enter and the room's stable identity. It does not preserve a
live room or membership. Redeeming it answers a fresh entrance reference, and
the first successful subscription reconstitutes the room after the last
membership leaves or the server restarts. Nothing in a link says what it opens,
so a private room's link says nothing about the room's label, and the label says
nothing about the link.

# An introduction, call by call

A direct conversation is the introduction OCapN calls a three-party handoff.
Here it is as the calls that make it, with Mira asking to message the lead
bot:

| Step | Who calls | Call | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Both, earlier | `register` on the directory, then `offerInbox` | Each has a name and has offered the server an inbox for introductions |
| 2 | Mira | `introduce` on the directory, naming the bot | The server hands Mira a reference to the inbox the bot offered |
| 3 | Mira's peer | Redeems that reference with the bot's peer | A connection opens between the two peers. The server is not on it |
| 4 | Mira | `dm` on the bot's inbox, with her own reply inbox alongside | The first line arrives, and the bot's replies come back to that inbox. When Mira's side cannot be dialed, `directReady` hands the reply inbox over instead |
| 5 | Either | `handRoom`, `offerRoom`, `handArchive`, `offerArchive` | References move between them over the same connection, as live handoffs or as durable links |

When the two peers are browsers, WebRTC's connection details travel through
the directory as `offerSignaling`, `signalOffer`, `signalAnswer`, and
`signalCandidate`, until the two are talking and the server drops out.

# Files, call by call

| Step | Call | On | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `attach` | your seat | One **upload** reference per file |
| 2 | `postFiles` | your seat | The line is posted with its file records, and every member gets a **blob** per file at once |
| 3 | `write`, chunk by chunk | the upload | Bytes arrive at the room |
| 4 | `read` with offset and length | the blob | A member reads; a read that reaches bytes still on their way in waits for them |

In a direct conversation the sender hosts the blob on the direct connection
and the other side reads it the same way. The room never sees those bytes.

# What OCapN gives Rooms

- **No ambient authority.** There is no account table and no permission
  check by name anywhere in the tables above. If you can form the call, you
  may make it, because forming it means holding the reference.
- **Introductions across three parties.** The server can hand you a
  reference to something another peer hosts, and your peer redeems it with
  them directly. Direct messages, invitations, and shared archives are all
  this one move.
- **Promises.** A call answers a promise, so a client can keep sending to a
  room while its `open` is still on the way back.
- **Durable links.** A sturdyref names behavior that a Host can reconstitute
  across restarts and connections. A room link reconstitutes an entrance, not
  the prior live room or membership. Room links, invitations, and admin secrets
  are all sturdyrefs.

The vocabulary is written once and shared. `lib/protocol.ts` in the
repository is the readable specification, `zig build gen-rooms-protocol`
generates the core verbs into each client, and clients in Zig, Rust, Python,
Racket, TypeScript, and the browser all speak it. So does a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/) program: it can sit in a
room and answer a direct message, with nothing in Goblins changed to allow
it, because the protocol is only OCapN.

Every verb above, with the reasoning behind it, lives beside the code:
[`protocol.ts`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol.ts)
for the directory, rooms, seats, lines, files, and introductions, and its
siblings for
[handover](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_handover.ts),
[IRC](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_irc.ts),
and the
[admin objects](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/lib/protocol_operator.ts).
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) shows how the code that
speaks it is divided.
